<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cripps Mission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cripps Mission was a British attempt in March and April 1942 to secure Indian cooperation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> Cripps offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> dominion status and an elected constitution-making body after the war, with the right of provinces to stay out. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> both rejected the proposals, and the failure led Congress to launch the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quit-india-movement">Quit India Movement</a> in August 1942.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in September 1939, Viceroy Linlithgow declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> a belligerent on Britain's side without consulting Indian leaders or the elected provincial representatives. The Congress provincial governments resigned en masse, while the All India Muslim League, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a> and regional parties supported the war effort in exchange for concessions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s entry into the war in December 1941, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-singapore">fall of Singapore</a> on 15 February 1942 and the retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> made an invasion of India a real threat.</p><p>The War Cabinet was divided. Labour ministers and moderate Conservatives wanted progress towards self-government, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>, supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a>, Leo Amery, opposed any dismantling of the empire. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which needed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as a base for aid to Chiang Kai Shek's Nationalist China, pressed for a settlement, and by 9 March 1942 the cabinet had agreed to send the mission. Cripps landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> on 22 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Cripps, a member of the Labour Party and a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, held talks with Congress leaders and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a>. Chakravarti Rajagopalachari, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Vallabhbhai Patel</a>, Maulana Azad and Nehru, offered full support in return for immediate self-government, but in public Cripps could offer little in the short term beyond more Indian members of the Viceroy's Executive Council. Gandhi called the offer of dominion status after the war a post-dated cheque drawn on a failing bank. Jinnah objected that the proposals made no clear concession for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In August 1942 the Congress working committee threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a> unless its Quit India call was conceded, and the British imprisoned the Congress leadership for the rest of the war. Jinnah's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> condemned the movement, and encouraged Muslims to join the war effort. Historians give two main reasons for the mission's failure: its promises were to be kept only after the war, and Linlithgow and Amery worked against it behind the scenes, as correspondence released in 1970 shows. After the war even Churchill accepted that the offer of independence could not be withdrawn, and the Labour government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a> granted India independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cripps Mission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Cripps Mission</b> failed, the Congress launched the Quit India Movement on 8 August 1942, and Gandhi called on Indians to “do or die”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Cripps Mission</b> of March 1942 was sent to win total co-operation during the war in return for devolution of power to an elected Indian legislature.</blockquote><a href="/movement/quit-india-movement">Quit India movement · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
